                                             (it finished green; see the update below)

== the Windows failure, step by step

  7 Provide ripgrep for the search tools: failure
  8 cargo test (target-OS crates):          skipped

Nothing of the product was executed. The step that failed is the one that downloads
the pinned ripgrep the search tools need in a debug build.

  D:\a\_temp\2e84eeab-90d5-47f4-80f9-f1e2f9b7bd38.sh: line 40:
      /d/a/_temp/ripgrep-15.0.0/rg.exe: No such file or directory
  ##[error]Process completed with exit code 127.

== cause

The Windows branch downloaded `ripgrep-15.0.0-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip` and ran
`Expand-Archive -DestinationPath .`, then executed `$tools_dir/rg.exe`. The archive
holds its files under a top-level `ripgrep-15.0.0-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc/` directory,
so `rg.exe` landed one level down and the path the step used had never existed. The
tarball branch was unaffected because `tar --strip-components=1` flattens that same
directory.

`scripts/test-platform.ps1`, which does the same job for a developer-run Windows
check, already handled this with `Move-Item`; only the inline workflow step was wrong.

== the fix, and what was checked here

The workflow step now moves the binary out of the archive's directory the way
`--strip-components=1` does for the tarball, and fails with a directory listing if
the binary is not at the path it is about to export, instead of exit 127 from an
unexplained exec.

Reproduced locally (Ubuntu 22.04) by taking the step's own script text out of
`.github/workflows/ci.yml` and running its Windows branch, substituting only the
extraction primitive -- PowerShell's `Expand-Archive` for `python3 -m zipfile -e`,
which writes the same tree:

  rg.exe at the path the step exports: True
  PE binary: True bytes: 4265472
  moved out of the archive's directory: True
  exit: 126  cannot execute binary file: Exec format error

That exit 126 is this Linux machine declining to *run* a Windows binary and is
expected; it is the proof the file is now where the step says it is. Before the fix
the same run failed earlier with 127 "No such file or directory", which is the error
the Windows runner reported.

